      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">7. Top 4 Consular Failure Modes & Mitigation Runbook</h2>
        <p>
          Historical rejection data indicates that over 80% of consular holdbacks result from four remediable administrative errors:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">1. Bank Statement Currency & Inbound Mismatches:</strong> Submitting statements from fintech neo-banks where transaction descriptions lack payer identification or reflect high volatility below €3,480. <em>Remedy:</em> Submit certified 90-day statements from regulated domestic commercial banks accompanied by foreign exchange conversion certificates.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">2. Lack of Finanças Lease Registration Stamp:</strong> Providing a private rental contract that was not registered online with Finanças. <em>Remedy:</em> Request landlord provide the official declaration <em>Comprovativo de Modelo 2</em> generated by the Portal das Finanças showing payment of 10% stamp duty (<em>Imposto do Selo</em>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">3. Criminal Background Check Expiration & Missing Hague Apostille:</strong> Submitting state-level police checks without an FBI Identity History Summary (US) or ACRO certificate (UK), or checks exceeding the 90-day consular validity window. <em>Remedy:</em> Order federal-level background documents directly with expedited Hague Apostille within 45 days of filing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">4. Ambiguous Remote Telework Contracts:</strong> Submitting employment agreements that fail to explicitly authorize international remote telework from Portugal. <em>Remedy:</em> Have HR sign an addendum confirming unrestricted cross-border remote work permissions without triggering local branch liabilities.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 bg-slate-900/40">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">Can I transition from the D8 Temporary Stay visa to a Portuguese Residence Permit?</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              No. The Temporary Stay Visa (up to 1 year) cannot be renewed into a multi-year residence card within Portugal. Applicants seeking the 5-year naturalization path must apply specifically for the D8 Residency Visa (<em>Visto de Residência</em>) at their home consulate.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 bg-slate-900/40">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">Are cryptocurrency earnings or capital dividends acceptable for D8 income proof?</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Passive dividends and capital gains are categorized under the D7 visa stream. The D8 specifically targets remote active work (contracting or salaried telework). If your income derives purely from liquid assets or staking yields, consulates mandate applying under the D7 passive income regime.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 bg-slate-900/40">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">How long does AIMA take to issue the physical biometric residence card?</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Upon arriving in Portugal on the 120-day double-entry visa, applicants attend their scheduled AIMA biometric appointment. Physical residence cards typically arrive via certified CTT mail within 30 to 75 business days following the biometric interview.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>

  <script is:inline>
    function updateD8Calc() {
      const smn = 870;
      const adultDeps = parseInt(document.getElementById('adult-deps').value) || 0;
      const childDeps = parseInt(document.getElementById('child-deps').value) || 0;
      const userIncome = parseFloat(document.getElementById('user-income').value) || 0;

      const monthlyReq = (smn * 4) + (adultDeps * smn * 0.5) + (childDeps * smn * 0.3);
      const savingsReq = (smn * 12) + (adultDeps * smn * 6) + (childDeps * smn * 3.6);

      document.getElementById('res-monthly').innerText = '€' + Math.round(monthlyReq).toLocaleString();
      document.getElementById('res-deposit').innerText = '€' + Math.round(savingsReq).toLocaleString();

      const statusEl = document.getElementById('res-status');
      if (userIncome >= monthlyReq) {
        const pct = Math.round((userIncome / monthlyReq) * 100);
        statusEl.innerText = `QUALIFIED (${pct}%)`;
        statusEl.className = 'text-lg font-bold text-emerald-400 font-mono mt-1';
      } else {
        const deficit = Math.round(monthlyReq - userIncome);
        statusEl.innerText = `DEFICIT (-€${deficit})`;
        statusEl.className = 'text-lg font-bold text-rose-400 font-mono mt-1';
      }
    }

    document.getElementById('adult-deps')?.addEventListener('input', updateD8Calc);
    document.getElementById('child-deps')?.addEventListener('input', updateD8Calc);
    document.getElementById('user-income')?.addEventListener('input', updateD8Calc);
  </script>
</Layout>
